🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘁𝘂𝗰𝗸 𝗶𝗻 𝗣𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗳𝘁𝗲𝗿 𝗦𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You increased the replicas of a Kubernetes Deployment, but the new pods are stuck in Pending. Existing pods are working fine. How will you troubleshoot it?”

This is a common production scenario.

I wouldn't touch the existing healthy pods first.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Identify which pods are Pending.

2️⃣ Describe the Pending pod

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

The Events section is usually the first place I check.

Look for:

🔹 Insufficient CPU/Memory
🔹 Node selector/affinity issues
🔹 Taints and tolerations
🔹 PVC/storage problems
🔹 Scheduling constraints

3️⃣ Check node capacity

kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe nodes

I would compare the pod's resource requests with the available allocatable resources.

4️⃣ Check resource requests

A pod may remain Pending even when a node appears to have free resources if the requested CPU/memory cannot be satisfied.

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"

5️⃣ Check scheduling rules

I would verify:

🔹 nodeSelector
🔹 Node affinity
🔹 Taints & tolerations
🔹 Pod affinity/anti-affinity

6️⃣ Check storage

If the pod requires persistent storage:

kubectl get pvc -n <namespace>

A PVC that cannot be bound can prevent the workload from being scheduled successfully.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Pending Pod → Events → Node Capacity → Resource Requests → Scheduling Rules → Storage → Root Cause

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Scaling a Deployment doesn't guarantee that every new replica can be scheduled.

Existing pods may be healthy while new replicas remain Pending because of capacity or scheduling constraints.
